      "text": "Advatages of Spring framework\n============================",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Advatages of Spring framework\n============================",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "qxoM2cUQQ_zWf4lHf--fe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 417,
      "y": 755,
      "width": 657,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 371448103,
      "version": 381,
      "versionNonce": 53545225,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785045065982,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Loose coupling\n \n\n     Coupling is the degree of dependency between two classes or \n     modules.\n     coupling -> If the object of one class depends on the object\n                   of another class to perform its functionality, \n                   then those two classes are said to be coupled.\n     \n     ex-> class Car need class Engine\n\n     Loose coupling means that one class depends on an \n     abstraction (interface or abstract class) instead of a \n     specific implementation. This reduces the dependency between \n     classes, making the code flexible and easy to maintain.\n            ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Loose coupling\n \n\n     Coupling is the degree of dependency between two classes or \n     modules.\n     coupling -> If the object of one class depends on the object\n                   of another class to perform its functionality, \n                   then those two classes are said to be coupled.\n     \n     ex-> class Car need class Engine\n\n     Loose coupling means that one class depends on an \n     abstraction (interface or abstract class) instead of a \n     specific implementation. This reduces the dependency between \n     classes, making the code flexible and easy to maintain.\n            ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
